/// \defgroup grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists Gadget helpers: list box, combo box text, text entry, buttons and check boxes
/// \ingroup grp_gui_apt
/// \brief Free functions that fill, measure, scroll and recolour list
///        boxes, read and write combo box and text entry text, filter typed
///        characters, and query push buttons and check boxes.
///
/// This group continues \ref grp_w1_gui_gadgets, which explains what a
/// gadget is (a GameWindow with a gadget style bit and a block of
/// gadget-specific user data) and how its input and system callbacks are
/// reached. The functions here are the same kind of small C-style API,
/// called by the window manager's gadget factories, by GameWindow when a
/// font change meets a list box or combo box style bit, by
/// GameWindowManager::assignDefaultGadgetLook when it gives a new gadget its
/// default colours, and above all by screen code: the control bar and
/// the Apt screen callbacks (save and load, options, lobbies, chat, the
/// multiplayer setup screens).
///
/// \par The list box model
/// Zero Hour calls the list box's user data ListboxData. BFME 2's block is
/// laid out differently (its row records are larger, for example), so Zero
/// Hour's header does not describe it exactly. The model the functions below
/// rely on:
/// - The list is an array of rows with a fixed capacity (the list length)
///   and a count of rows in use. Each row has one cell per column; a cell
///   holds either text (a DisplayString, so it can wrap and be measured) or
///   an image.
/// - Every row stores its height and the running total of all row heights
///   up to and including it, which is the row's bottom edge in list pixels.
///   computeTotalHeight() recomputes both for every row, and the list's
///   total height; an overwriting text add updates them for its own row
///   only (see GadgetListBoxAddEntryText()).
/// - The visible area is a display height in pixels and a display position,
///   the pixel offset of the visible area from the top of the list.
///   Scrolling changes only the display position; the scroll bar (a slider
///   with an up and a down button, all child windows of the list box)
///   follows it.
/// - Entries are added and read by sending the list box system messages:
///   GadgetListBoxAddEntryText() sends an add request and
///   GadgetListBoxGetTextAndColor() a get-text request. The list box's own
///   system callback does the work.
///
/// \par Combo box children
/// A combo box owns a drop-down button, an edit box (a text entry field)
/// and a list box, kept in that order in its data in both games. Some BFME 2
/// bodies fetch these children through small accessor functions whose code
/// matches Zero Hour's GadgetComboBoxGetEditBox and GadgetComboBoxGetListBox
/// as compiled with Zero Hour's field offsets. BFME 2 places the three children
/// one pointer slot later, so in BFME 2 the accessor Zero Hour names for the
/// edit box reads the drop-down button, and the one it names for the list
/// box reads the edit box (see also GadgetComboBoxSetColors()). The
/// descriptions below name each child by the role the body gives it.
///
/// GadgetComboBoxSetMaxDisplay() and GadgetButtonGetData() belong to this
/// topic but are defined in GadgetComboBox.cpp and GadgetPushButton.cpp;
/// their entries appear on those files' pages under
/// \ref grp_w1_gui_gadgets.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The bodies come from Zero Hour's GadgetListBox.cpp, GadgetComboBox.cpp,
/// GadgetTextEntry.cpp, GadgetPushButton.cpp, GadgetCheckBox.cpp,
/// GadgetSlider.h (the two slider thumb getters, inline there) and
/// W3DPushButton.cpp, most of them through the BFME 1 reconstruction.
/// GadgetTextEntryValidateCharacter() has no Zero Hour counterpart. Where a
/// function entry notes a difference between BFME 2 and Zero Hour, the note
/// rests on both bodies.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - None of these functions reads or writes game-logic state; they operate
///   on window data. Their results reach the game only through the screens
///   that call them (for example the control bar's command buttons and the
///   multiplayer setup and rules screens), so a change here can alter what a
///   player selects or orders but is not itself part of the logic
///   simulation.
/// - Data: a .wnd text entry's TEXTENTRYDATA block gives its maximum
///   length, whether it is secret (masked), and three input filters
///   (numbers only, letters and numbers only, ASCII only), which BFME 2's
///   parser stores as bits that GadgetTextEntryValidateCharacter() then
///   tests. A COMBOBOXDATA block gives, among other values, whether the
///   combo box is editable and how many rows its list shows (roles from
///   Zero Hour and BFME 1; see \ref grp_w1_gui_gadgets), which reach
///   GadgetComboBoxSetIsEditable() and GadgetComboBoxSetMaxDisplay(). Which
///   BFME 2 screens still load .wnd layouts is not established here.
/// - Fixed in code: typed Thai characters are refused by text entry
///   fields; a list box counts as full when its bottom visible row ends no
///   more than 5 pixels above the visible area's bottom edge (or below it);
///   the default list box and combo box colours are constants in
///   GameWindowManager::assignDefaultGadgetLook.
/// - The text shown in list boxes and combo boxes is whatever the calling
///   screen passes in (GameState::populateSaveGameListbox, for example,
///   adds a string-table entry for its new-save line), so wording lives in
///   the string table or in the screen code, not here.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - All sizes are whole pixels. Row heights are measured in two places.
///   addEntry, the list box's internal routine for adding text, handles an
///   overwriting add by measuring only the new cell's display string, and
///   it only ever grows the row: shorter text leaves the row's height as it
///   was, and rows below keep their bottom edges. That is the path screens
///   normally take when they append: Zero Hour's declaration defaults
///   overwrite to true, and BFME 2 callers such as GadgetComboBoxSystem and
///   AptDisconnectScreen::InitGadgets pass true.
///   computeTotalHeight() re-measures every row. addEntry runs it for a
///   non-overwriting add; addImageEntry, GadgetListBoxSetListLength and the
///   list box's system callback run it too (Zero Hour's callback does so
///   when an entry is deleted, when the buffer scrolls and when the list box
///   is resized). GadgetListBoxSetFont() changes the font of existing cells
///   without recomputing row heights. Nothing here scales for resolution.
///   UI scaling has to come from the fonts and window sizes the screens
///   create.
/// - Text is UnicodeString, 16-bit characters; the character filter tests
///   one 16-bit unit at a time with the C runtime's wide-character
///   classification functions.
/// - Only W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDraw() is in the W3D device layer, but
///   list box layout still depends on the renderer. Row heights come from
///   two sources: the measured size of a cell's DisplayString (Zero Hour:
///   implemented by W3DDisplayString) and the window manager's font height
///   for the list box's font. Zero Hour: that font height is
///   GameFont::height, which W3DFontLibrary fills when it loads the font.
///   computeTotalHeight() gives a text cell the font height when the list
///   box is a one-line list and the measured size otherwise, but an
///   overwriting add always uses the measured size, so a one-line list's
///   rows can change height when a full re-measure runs. A new renderer
///   must therefore supply both text measurement and font heights, and keep
///   them consistent with each other.
///
/// \see \ref sub_gui_apt, \ref grp_w1_gui_gadgets


/// \file GUI/GadgetListBoxAddEntryText.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists
/// \brief Adding text to a list box: GadgetListBoxAddEntryText().
///
/// The list box internals it relies on (the add-entry handler, the
/// bottom-row search) live in other files.


/// \fn int GadgetListBoxAddEntryText(GameWindow *listbox, UnicodeString text, int color, int row, int column, bool overwrite)
/// \brief Adds a line of text to a list box, or writes text into an
///        existing cell, and keeps an auto-scrolling list on its newest
///        line.
///
/// This is how screens fill list boxes. Retail callers include
/// GameState::populateSaveGameListbox (the save and load lists),
/// positionStartSpots (which in BFME 2 also fills a list box),
/// GadgetComboBoxSystem (a combo box adding to its own list) and
/// AptDisconnectScreen::InitGadgets.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Empty text is replaced by a single space before anything else.
/// - The function builds an add request (row, column, a pointer to the
///   text, the text cell type, the overwrite flag, and -1 for the width and
///   height) and sends it to the list box as the add-entry system message
///   together with the colour. The list box's system callback inserts the
///   entry and returns its row index, which is passed back to the caller.
/// - Row height: the callback hands a text add to addEntry, a list box
///   internal. Its row-height logic, described here from the BFME 2 body,
///   follows Zero Hour's (BFME 2's row and cell records are larger, and it
///   also clears one cell field that Zero Hour's cell does not have).
///   Without overwrite, addEntry re-measures the whole list with
///   computeTotalHeight(). With overwrite, it measures only the new cell's
///   display string; when that is taller than the row, it raises the row's
///   height, its bottom edge (one pixel more for a newly created row, the
///   gap between rows) and the list's total height, then realigns the
///   display position with the top visible row and refreshes the scroll
///   bar. Shorter text never shrinks a row, and the rows below an
///   overwritten row keep their old bottom edges.
/// - Auto-scroll: when the list box has its scroll-if-at-end option set,
///   the new row is the one right after the previous bottom visible row (or
///   the same index, when the list was already at its row capacity), and
///   the visible area is now full (GadgetListBoxIsFull()), the list scrolls
///   so that the new row is the bottom visible one
///   (GadgetListBoxSetBottomVisibleEntry()). A list the user has scrolled
///   back through therefore stays where it is; one showing its end follows
///   new lines, as a chat window should.
/// - The steps match Zero Hour's, except that the empty-text test reads
///   the string's length directly (Zero Hour's tests the first character).
///
/// \param listbox   The list box. Null returns -1. Its user data must be
///                  the list box data; nothing checks it.
/// \param text      The text, passed by value.
/// \param color     Text colour.
/// \param row       Row to write. Zero Hour: -1, or any row at or past the
///                  list's insert position, appends at the insert position.
/// \param column    Column to write. Zero Hour: -1 means column 0.
/// \param overwrite true writes into the cell of an existing row; false,
///                  when the row already has cells, pushes it and the rows
///                  after it down and inserts a new row. A row without cells
///                  gets new cells either way. It also decides how the row
///                  height is measured (see above). Screens normally append
///                  with true.
/// \return The row index the list box reports, or -1 for a null list box.
///         Zero Hour: the list box also returns -1 when the column is at or
///         past its column count, or when the list is at its capacity, does
///         not auto-purge, and the call would add a row rather than
///         overwrite an existing one.
///
/// \see GadgetListBoxGetText(), computeTotalHeight()


/// \file Gadget/GadgetListBoxIsFull.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists
/// \brief The list box fullness test, GadgetListBoxIsFull().


/// \fn bool GadgetListBoxIsFull(GameWindow *window)
/// \brief Reports whether a list box's rows fill its visible area.
///
/// The test behind auto-scrolling: GadgetListBoxAddEntryText(), its only
/// retail caller, scrolls to a new line only once the list is full, so a
/// short list does not scroll at all.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Finds the bottom visible row, with the same search that Zero Hour calls
/// getListboxBottomEntry, and returns true when that row's bottom edge
/// reaches the display position plus the display height, minus 5 pixels.
/// The logic is Zero Hour's; BFME 2's row record is larger.
///
/// \param window The list box. Null gives false.
/// \return true when the visible area is full; false when it is not, or
///         when the window or its user data is null.
///
/// \see GadgetListBoxSetBottomVisibleEntry()


/// \file Gadget/GadgetListBoxGetText.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists
/// \brief Reading list box cells: GadgetListBoxGetTextAndColor() and its wrapper
///        GadgetListBoxGetText().
///
/// Zero Hour GadgetListBox.cpp code, carried over through BFME 1.


/// \fn UnicodeString GadgetListBoxGetTextAndColor(GameWindow *listbox, Color *color, Int row, Int column)
/// \brief Returns the text and colour of one list box cell.
///
/// Sends the list box a get-text system message with the cell's position
/// and receives a copy of the cell's text and its colour; the list box's
/// system callback fills them in. Retail callers are
/// GadgetListBoxGetText() and GadgetComboBoxSystem.
///
/// \param listbox The list box.
/// \param color   [out] The cell's colour. Set to 0 first, so it is 0 when
///                the function returns early. Must not be null.
/// \param row     Row index; -1 returns the empty string.
/// \param column  Column index; -1 returns the empty string.
/// \return A copy of the cell's text. The empty string when the list box
///         is null, the row or column is -1, or the window does not have
///         the list box style bit.
///
/// \see GadgetListBoxGetText(), GadgetListBoxAddEntryText()


/// \fn UnicodeString GadgetListBoxGetText(GameWindow *listbox, Int row, Int column)
/// \brief Returns the text of one list box cell, discarding its colour.
///
/// A wrapper around GadgetListBoxGetTextAndColor() with a local colour
/// variable. Retail callers include the map preview code and the in-game
/// chat windows.
///
/// \param listbox The list box.
/// \param row     Row index.
/// \param column  Column index.
/// \return A copy of the cell's text, or the empty string as described for
///         GadgetListBoxGetTextAndColor().


/// \file Gadget/GadgetListBoxSetBottomVisibleEntry.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists
/// \brief Scrolling a list box to a row: GadgetListBoxSetBottomVisibleEntry().


/// \fn void GadgetListBoxSetBottomVisibleEntry(GameWindow *window, Int newPos)
/// \brief Scrolls a list box so that a given row ends at the bottom of the
///        visible area.
///
/// GadgetListBoxAddEntryText() calls it to keep an auto-scrolling list on
/// its newest line; that is its only retail caller, so in practice it
/// scrolls forward. The display position is set outright, not stepped, so
/// a list already scrolled further down would move back.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Returns at once when the window or its user data is null, or when
///   newPos is negative.
/// - Returns without scrolling when the row's bottom edge is within the
///   display height, that is, when the row would be visible with the list
///   at its top. The display position is not reset in that case.
/// - Otherwise sets the display position to the row's bottom edge minus
///   the display height plus one, then refreshes the scroll bar's range and
///   position to match.
///
/// \note This differs from both Zero Hour and BFME 1, whose version finds
///       the current bottom visible row and moves the display by the
///       difference in rows. BFME 2 computes the pixel position directly
///       from the row's bottom edge.
///
/// \param window The list box.
/// \param newPos Index of the row to bring to the bottom.
///
/// \warning newPos is not checked against the number of rows; an index
///          past the row array reads beyond it.
///
/// \see GadgetListBoxIsFull()


/// \file Gadget/GadgetListBoxComputeTotalHeight.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists
/// \brief List box row layout: computeTotalHeight().


/// \fn void computeTotalHeight(GameWindow *window)
/// \brief Recomputes every row's height and bottom edge and the list box's
///        total height, then updates its scroll bar.
///
/// Called after the rows change. Its retail callers are addEntry (adding
/// text, but only when the add does not overwrite; an overwriting add
/// measures its one cell itself, see GadgetListBoxAddEntryText()),
/// addImageEntry (adding an image), GadgetListBoxSetListLength and the list
/// box's system callback, GadgetListBoxSystem (Zero Hour's runs it when an
/// entry is deleted, when the buffer scrolls and when the list box is
/// resized). The heights it writes are what scrolling works from, for
/// example in GadgetListBoxIsFull() and GadgetListBoxSetBottomVisibleEntry()
/// (see the list box model in the group description).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// For each row in use that has cells, the row height is the tallest of
/// its cells:
/// - a text cell is one font height when the window has the status bit
///   Zero Hour calls WIN_STATUS_ONE_LINE, and otherwise its display string's
///   measured height (so wrapped text makes a taller row);
/// - an image cell is its own height plus one when it has one, and
///   otherwise one font height.
///
/// Font heights come from the window manager for the window's font. Each
/// row's bottom edge is the running total of row heights, counting one
/// extra pixel per row as a gap, and the last total becomes the list's
/// total height. Rows without cells are skipped and keep their old values.
/// Finally the scroll bar's range and position are refreshed, with the same
/// scroll bar refresh that GadgetListBoxSetBottomVisibleEntry() uses.
///
/// \note Zero Hour declares this function static in GadgetListBox.cpp and
///       ends it with adjustDisplay, which also moves the display position
///       to the start of the top visible row. The BFME 2 helper's own body
///       updates the scroll bar and does not write the display position.
///
/// \param window The list box; its user data must be the list box data.
///
/// \see GadgetListBoxSetFont(), GadgetListBoxAddEntryText()


/// \file Gadget/GadgetListBoxSetFont.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists
/// \brief The list box font setter, GadgetListBoxSetFont().


/// \fn void GadgetListBoxSetFont(GameWindow *g, GameFont *font)
/// \brief Applies a font to a list box's own text and tooltip and to the
///        text of every cell.
///
/// GameWindow::winSetFont stores the font on the window and then, for a
/// window with the list box style, calls this so that existing entries
/// change font as well. It is the list box counterpart of
/// GadgetTextEntrySetFont() and GadgetComboBoxSetFont().
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Sets the font on the window's text and tooltip display strings when
/// they exist. Then, when the list box data exists, it walks every row up
/// to the list's capacity and every column, and sets the font on each text
/// cell that has a display string. Rows without cells are skipped. The
/// display strings' font setter is called through its virtual table slot.
/// Row heights are not recomputed here. The logic is Zero Hour's; BFME 2's
/// row record is larger.
///
/// \param g    The list box; must not be null.
/// \param font Font to apply.
///
/// \see computeTotalHeight()


/// \file Gadget/GadgetListBoxSetColors.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists
/// \brief The list box colour setter, GadgetListBoxSetColors().


/// \fn void GadgetListBoxSetColors(GameWindow *listbox, Color enabledColor, Color enabledBorderColor, Color enabledSelectedItemColor, Color enabledSelectedItemBorderColor, Color disabledColor, Color disabledBorderColor, Color disabledSelectedItemColor, Color disabledSelectedItemBorderColor, Color hiliteColor, Color hiliteBorderColor, Color hiliteSelectedItemColor, Color hiliteSelectedItemBorderColor)
/// \brief Sets a list box's twelve state colours and passes matching
///        colours on to its scroll bar and scroll buttons.
///
/// Retail callers: GameWindowManager::assignDefaultGadgetLook, which gives
/// new list boxes the default palette, and GadgetComboBoxSetColors(),
/// which uses it for a combo box's list.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The list box: for each state (enabled, disabled, highlighted),
///   draw-data entry 0 gets the fill and border colours and entry 1 the
///   selected-item pair.
/// - When the list box has a scroll bar slider: the slider's entry 0 copies
///   the list box's fill and border colours for each state. The up and
///   down buttons copy the slider's entry 0 colours; for their entry 1 (the
///   pressed look) they take the slider thumb's selected colour, read with
///   GadgetSliderGetEnabledSelectedThumbBorderColor and its disabled and
///   highlighted siblings, and a second thumb colour value, read by
///   separate getters, in the place where Zero Hour uses the thumb's
///   selected border colour.
///
/// Zero Hour does the same with inline accessors; BFME 2 retail calls
/// separate getter functions for the thumb colours.
///
/// \param listbox                         The list box; its user data must
///                                        be the list box data.
/// \param enabledColor                    Fill colour when enabled.
/// \param enabledBorderColor              Border colour when enabled.
/// \param enabledSelectedItemColor        Selected-item colour when enabled.
/// \param enabledSelectedItemBorderColor  Selected-item border when enabled.
/// \param disabledColor                   Fill colour when disabled.
/// \param disabledBorderColor             Border colour when disabled.
/// \param disabledSelectedItemColor       Selected-item colour when disabled.
/// \param disabledSelectedItemBorderColor Selected-item border when disabled.
/// \param hiliteColor                     Fill colour when highlighted.
/// \param hiliteBorderColor               Border colour when highlighted.
/// \param hiliteSelectedItemColor         Selected-item colour when
///                                        highlighted.
/// \param hiliteSelectedItemBorderColor   Selected-item border when
///                                        highlighted.
///
/// \warning When a slider is present, the up and down buttons are used
///          without a null check.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The scroll bar has no colours of its own here: it always follows the
/// list box, and its buttons follow the slider and its thumb.
///
/// \see GadgetSliderGetDisabledSelectedThumbBorderColor(),
///      GadgetComboBoxSetColors()


/// \file Gadget/GadgetSliderSelectedThumbBorderColors.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists
/// \brief Two slider thumb colour getters used when a list box is recoloured.
///
/// Zero Hour defines these as inline functions in GadgetSlider.h; BFME 2
/// retail has out-of-line copies. The enabled-state sibling is defined in
/// another file.


/// \fn Int GadgetSliderGetDisabledSelectedThumbBorderColor(GameWindow *slider)
/// \brief Returns a colour of a slider's thumb for the disabled pressed
///        look, or the undefined colour when the slider has no thumb.
///
/// The thumb is the slider's child window. Its sole retail caller is
/// GadgetListBoxSetColors(), which gives the value to the scroll buttons as
/// their disabled selected colour.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The value read is the colour field of the thumb's second disabled
/// draw-data entry, not its border colour, despite the name. That reading
/// comes from the draw-data layout that GadgetListBoxSetColors() itself
/// establishes, and it agrees with how that function uses the result: Zero
/// Hour fills the same slot from GadgetSliderGetDisabledSelectedThumbColor.
/// The enabled-state sibling, GadgetSliderGetEnabledSelectedThumbBorderColor,
/// reads its state's colour field in the same way.
///
/// \param slider The slider; must not be null.
/// \return The thumb's colour, or the undefined colour when the slider has
///         no child: the value Zero Hour calls GAME_COLOR_UNDEFINED, white
///         with zero alpha.
///
/// \see GadgetSliderGetHiliteSelectedThumbBorderColor()


/// \fn Int GadgetSliderGetHiliteSelectedThumbBorderColor(GameWindow *slider)
/// \brief Returns a colour of a slider's thumb for the highlighted pressed
///        look, or the undefined colour when the slider has no thumb.
///
/// The highlighted-state twin of
/// GadgetSliderGetDisabledSelectedThumbBorderColor(); the same remarks about
/// the field read and its only caller, GadgetListBoxSetColors(), apply.
///
/// \param slider The slider; must not be null.
/// \return The thumb's colour, or the undefined colour when the slider has
///         no child.


/// \fn void GadgetComboBoxSetMaxDisplay(GameWindow *comboBox, Int maxDisplay)
/// \brief Sets how many rows a combo box's drop-down list shows before it
///        needs its scroll bar.
///
/// Only stores the value in the combo box data; Zero Hour's comment on the
/// field describes it as the number of entries shown before the slider
/// appears. The combo box factory, GameWindowManager::gogoGadgetComboBox,
/// calls it while building a combo box; in Zero Hour it passes the value
/// from the combo box's creation data.
///
/// \param comboBox   The combo box; must not be null and must have its
///                   data.
/// \param maxDisplay Number of rows.
///
/// \par Modding
/// In Zero Hour the value comes from the rows-shown setting of a .wnd
/// file's COMBOBOXDATA block.
///
/// \see GadgetComboBoxSetIsEditable(), GadgetComboBoxSetMaxChars()


/// \file Gadget/GadgetComboBoxGetText.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists
/// \brief Reading a combo box's text: GadgetComboBoxGetText().


/// \fn UnicodeString GadgetComboBoxGetText(GameWindow *comboBox)
/// \brief Returns the text currently shown in a combo box's edit box.
///
/// Zero Hour: when the user picks a list entry, the combo box's system
/// callback copies that entry's text and colour into the edit box, so this
/// is the combo box's value whether it was typed or picked. Retail callers
/// include the multiplayer game setup screen and AptMpClans::Delete. The
/// logic is Zero Hour's. BFME 2 reaches the edit box through the accessor
/// whose code matches Zero Hour's GadgetComboBoxGetListBox (see Combo box
/// children in the group description); Zero Hour calls
/// GadgetComboBoxGetEditBox.
///
/// \param comboBox The combo box.
/// \return A copy of the edit box's text (read with
///         GadgetTextEntryGetText()); the empty string when the window is
///         null or does not have the combo box style bit.
///
/// \see GadgetComboBoxSetText()


/// \file Gadget/GadgetComboBoxSetText.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists
/// \brief Setting a combo box's text: GadgetComboBoxSetText().


/// \fn void GadgetComboBoxSetText(GameWindow *comboBox, UnicodeString text)
/// \brief Sets a combo box's list box selection to -1, then puts text into
///        its edit box.
///
/// Retail callers include the multiplayer game setup screen and the online
/// login screen.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's version only sets the edit box text. BFME 2, like
///       BFME 1, first calls GadgetListBoxSetSelected on the list box with
///       -1. Zero Hour: an out-of-range selection such as -1 clears the list
///       box's selection and notifies its owner.
///
/// \param comboBox The combo box. Null is ignored.
/// \param text     New text, passed by value; it is set with
///                 GadgetTextEntrySetText.
///
/// \see GadgetComboBoxGetText()


/// \file Gadget/GadgetComboBoxSetFont.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists
/// \brief The combo box font setter, GadgetComboBoxSetFont().


/// \fn void GadgetComboBoxSetFont(GameWindow *comboBox, GameFont *font)
/// \brief Applies a font to a combo box's list box and edit box and to its
///        own text and tooltip.
///
/// GameWindow::winSetFont calls it for a window with the combo box style.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The list box and then the edit box, when present, get the font through
/// GameWindow::winSetFont, which hands each one to its own gadget font
/// function (GadgetListBoxSetFont(), GadgetTextEntrySetFont()). Retail
/// calls the GameWindow implementation directly rather than through the
/// virtual table. Then the combo box's own text and tooltip display strings
/// get the font. The drop-down button is not changed. Zero Hour does the
/// same, through the virtual call.
///
/// \param comboBox The combo box. Null is ignored.
/// \param font     Font to apply.
///
/// \see GadgetListBoxSetFont()


/// \file Gadget/GadgetComboBoxSetIsEditable.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists
/// \brief Switching a combo box between editable and choose-only:
///        GadgetComboBoxSetIsEditable().


/// \fn void GadgetComboBoxSetIsEditable(GameWindow *comboBox, Bool isEditable)
/// \brief Lets the user type into a combo box, or restricts it to choosing
///        from its list.
///
/// Records the flag in the combo box data and changes the edit box's
/// window status. Retail callers include the combo box factory
/// (GameWindowManager::gogoGadgetComboBox) and the online screens.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// When the combo box has no edit box, nothing happens, including the
/// flag store. Otherwise editable clears, and not editable sets, two status
/// bits on the edit box, the ones Zero Hour calls WIN_STATUS_NO_INPUT and
/// WIN_STATUS_NO_FOCUS, using winClearStatus and winSetStatus.
///
/// \note Zero Hour changes only the no-input bit; its no-focus line is
///       commented out. Its editable branch also clears the bit in a copy
///       of the status and writes it back with winSetStatus, which only
///       adds bits, so in Zero Hour this function could not make a
///       read-only combo box editable again. BFME 2 calls winClearStatus
///       instead.
///
/// \param comboBox   The combo box; must not be null.
/// \param isEditable true to allow typing; false to allow only choosing.
///
/// \par Modding
/// In Zero Hour the factory passes the editable setting from a .wnd file's
/// COMBOBOXDATA block.
///
/// \see GadgetComboBoxSetMaxDisplay()


/// \file Gadget/GadgetTextEntryGetText.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists
/// \brief Reading a text entry field: GadgetTextEntryGetText().


/// \fn UnicodeString GadgetTextEntryGetText(GameWindow *textentry)
/// \brief Returns the text in a text entry field.
///
/// Sends the field the get-text system message with a string to fill; the
/// field's system callback copies its text into it. This is how screens
/// read what the player typed. Retail callers include
/// GadgetComboBoxGetText(), ControlBarSystem, AptInGameChat::Send,
/// AptOptions::Save, AptLanLobby::OnCreateGameBttn,
/// AptSkirmish::OnAddProfileAccept and
/// AptOnlineCustomMatch::AcceptJoinGame. The body is Zero Hour's.
///
/// \param textentry The text entry field.
/// \return A copy of the field's text; the empty string when the window is
///         null or does not have the entry field style bit. Zero Hour: for
///         a secret (password) field the callback copies the real text, not
///         the masked form.
///
/// \see GadgetTextEntryValidateCharacter(), GadgetTextEntrySetFont()


/// \file Gadget/GadgetTextEntryValidateCharacter.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists
/// \brief The text entry input filter, GadgetTextEntryValidateCharacter().


/// \fn bool GadgetTextEntryValidateCharacter(WideChar character, signed char flags)
/// \brief Decides whether a typed character may go into a text entry field,
///        according to the field's input filter bits.
///
/// GadgetTextEntryInsertCharacter calls it with the field's filter flags
/// before inserting a typed character, and refuses the character when this
/// returns false. Retail also uses it outside text fields: the
/// create-a-hero screen passes only the file-name bit and replaces each
/// refused character with a dash when it builds a picture file name from a
/// hero's name.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The checks run in this order:
/// - Characters U+0E01 to U+0E3A and U+0E3F to U+0E5B, which are all the
///   assigned characters of the Thai block, are always refused, whatever
///   the flags. Why is not recorded.
/// - 0x80: a space is accepted at once, skipping every later test.
/// - 0x01: a space is refused.
/// - 0x02: a percent sign is refused.
/// - 0x04: only printable ASCII from the double quote to the tilde is
///   allowed, and not the backslash; so space and the exclamation mark are
///   refused too unless 0x80 let the space through.
/// - 0x08: the characters Windows forbids in file names are refused:
///   asterisk, question mark, colon, backslash, slash, double quote, less
///   than, greater than and vertical bar.
/// - 0x10: the character must be ASCII (iswascii).
/// - 0x20: the character must be a digit (iswdigit).
/// - 0x40: the character must be a letter or a digit (iswalnum).
/// - Anything that survives is accepted.
///
/// The last three tests use the C runtime's wide-character classification
/// functions.
///
/// \param character The character, one 16-bit unit.
/// \param flags     Filter bits from the field's data, listed above.
/// \return true if the character may be inserted.
///
/// \par Modding
/// BFME 2's TEXTENTRYDATA parser sets 0x20, 0x40 and 0x10 from a .wnd
/// file's numbers-only, letters-and-numbers-only and ASCII-only values;
/// Zero Hour keeps those three options as separate fields instead. The
/// other bits are not set by that parser, and where they are set has not
/// been traced here. The Thai exclusion is hard-coded.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// A localisation that needs Thai input must remove the fixed range test.
///
/// \note Not a Zero Hour function. The name comes from the BFME 1
///       reconstruction and describes the body.
///
/// \see GadgetTextEntryGetText()


/// \fn void *GadgetButtonGetData(GameWindow *button)
/// \brief Returns the caller-defined pointer stored in a push button's
///        data.
///
/// Push buttons carry one free pointer for the screen that owns them.
/// Zero Hour: ControlBar::setControlCommand stores the button's
/// CommandButton there with GadgetButtonSetData, and the control bar reads
/// it back with this function to find out which command a button runs. In
/// BFME 2 the retail callers include ControlBar::update,
/// ControlBar::updateContextCommand, ControlBar::updateContextMultiSelect
/// and ControlBarObserverSystem. The body is Zero Hour's.
///
/// \param button The push button. Null gives null.
/// \return The stored pointer; null when the button or its data is null.
///
/// \see GadgetPushButtonSystem()


/// \file Gadget/GadgetPushButtonSystem.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists
/// \brief The push button system callback, GadgetPushButtonSystem().


/// \fn WindowMsgHandledType GadgetPushButtonSystem(GameWindow *window, UnsignedInt msg, WindowMsgData mData1, WindowMsgData mData2)
/// \brief System callback of the push button: sets its label, frees its
///        data and reports focus changes to its owner.
///
/// In BFME 2 the only direct retail call is from the system method of the
/// push button's window class, as for the other gadgets (see
/// \ref grp_w1_gui_gadgets). Retail also lists this function by name in
/// the function lexicon's table of system callbacks, as it lists
/// W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDraw() among the draw callbacks.
///
/// Messages handled:
/// - Set label: mData1 points to a UnicodeString, which becomes the
///   window's text. Retail calls the GameWindow implementation of
///   winSetText directly rather than through the virtual table.
/// - Create: nothing to do.
/// - Destroy: frees the button data and clears the window's user-data
///   pointer.
/// - Input focus: sets the highlighted state bit when the button gains
///   focus and clears it when it loses focus, sends the focus-change gadget
///   message to the window's owner with the new state and the button's
///   window id, and writes the new state to the Bool that mData2 points to.
///
/// \param window The push button.
/// \param msg    System message.
/// \param mData1 Label text pointer, or the focus state.
/// \param mData2 For input focus, a pointer to a Bool that receives the
///               focus state.
/// \return MSG_HANDLED for the four messages above; MSG_IGNORED otherwise.
///
/// \note BFME 2 push button data holds more than Zero Hour's: on destroy,
///       a display string kept in the data is first returned to the
///       display string manager, and deleting the data then releases a
///       reference-counted object that it holds. What that object is has
///       not been established. Zero Hour only deletes the data.
///
/// \see GadgetButtonGetData()


/// \file Gadget/GadgetCheckBoxIsChecked.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists
/// \brief The check box state query, GadgetCheckBoxIsChecked().


/// \fn Bool GadgetCheckBoxIsChecked(GameWindow *g)
/// \brief Reports whether a check box is ticked.
///
/// A check box keeps its ticked state in the selected bit of the window's
/// instance state, the bit Zero Hour calls WIN_STATE_SELECTED. Retail
/// callers include AptOptions::Save, which reads the options screen's check
/// boxes when settings are saved. The body is Zero Hour's, with the bit
/// test written as a shift.
///
/// \param g The check box. It is dereferenced without a null check.
/// \return true if the selected bit is set.
///
/// \see GadgetCheckLikeButtonIsChecked()


/// \file Gadget/W3DPushButtonImageDispatch_Thunk.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists
/// \brief W3D device layer: the image push button draw dispatcher,
///        W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDraw().
///
/// The single-image and horizontal three-piece routines it dispatches to
/// are in W3DPushButton.cpp; the vertical one is in
/// W3DPushButtonImageDrawVertical.cpp.


/// \fn void W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDraw(GameWindow *window, WinInstanceData *instData)
/// \brief Draw callback for image-based push buttons: picks which image
///        layout routine draws the button.
///
/// The retail function lexicon lists this function by name as a draw
/// callback, and the draw method of the push button's window class calls
/// it. It draws nothing itself.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - BFME 2 first tests a flag byte kept in the push button's data. When it
///   is set, a separate routine
///   draws the button as a vertical three-piece button: a top image, a
///   vertically tiled middle and a bottom image. Its BFME 2 body (in
///   W3DPushButtonImageDrawVertical.cpp, reconstructed from the BFME 1
///   donor) picks the three images from the disabled, highlighted or
///   enabled draw data by state, draws nothing unless all three exist,
///   tiles the middle between the top and bottom images (clipping the last
///   piece), and, when there is no room for a middle, splits the button's
///   height between the top and bottom images.
/// - Otherwise, as in Zero Hour: a button with a middle enabled image (the
///   horizontal three-piece look: left end, tiled middle, right end) is
///   drawn by W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDrawThree, unless it uses the global
///   overlay states, in which case W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDrawOne draws
///   it; a button without a middle image is drawn by
///   W3DGadgetPushButtonImageDrawOne as a single image.
/// - The overlay test reads the window's instance state word with the bit
///   value of the status flag Zero Hour calls WIN_STATUS_USE_OVERLAY_STATES;
///   Zero Hour's version does the same.
/// - The overlay-state branch still computes the button's screen position
///   and size and then ignores them. In Zero Hour they fed a debug-build
///   error message warning that overlay states are not supported for
///   three-piece buttons.
///
/// \param window   The push button.
/// \param instData The button's instance data: its images, state and image
///                 offset.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Which routine draws a button follows first from a flag in its push button
/// data: when it is set, the vertical routine draws the button whether or
/// not it has a middle enabled image, and it draws nothing unless the top,
/// middle and bottom images for the current state all exist. Otherwise the
/// draw data decides: a middle enabled image selects the horizontal
/// three-piece look, unless the button uses overlay states; without one, a
/// single image is drawn. Where the flag is set from data has not been
/// traced here.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// This is the push button's seam to the W3D renderer; a new renderer
/// replaces this function and the routines it calls.
///
/// \see GadgetPushButtonSystem()
